WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:09.570
On a sunny Tuesday afternoon, on the north
eastern outskirts of Paris, 100 passengers

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:15.549
began the boarding process onto the world’s
fastest commercial plane.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:20.760
Navigating down the narrow body of the Concordes
with pairs of business class seats flanking

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.020
them on each side.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:32.250
Flight 4590 was a chartered flight, carrying
the predominantly German passengers to embark

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:37.629
on an adventurous journey through the Caribbean,
the Panama Canal and finally Ecuador aboard

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:40.769
the MS Deutschland. [1]

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:45.870
The crew of the flight was composed of the
captain, a 53-year-old pilot with over 300

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.760
hours flown in the Concorde.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.860
The first officer was a 50-year-old with over
2,000 hours of Concorde flight time, and the

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.890
flight engineer who again accumulated over
900 hours of flying the Concorde.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.890
[2]

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:04.780
At 2:34 pm, Ground control gave clearance
for the flight to take off runway 26R.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:11.190
The longest runway of the Parisian airport.[3]
Which was needed to get the Concordes cumbersome

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.450
delta wings up to take-off speed.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:21.030
At 2:40 PM, flight Air France 4590 lined up
at the start of runway 26R.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.760
Beginning the exciting take-off sequence,
the world’s only commercial plane to employ

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.030
afterburners.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:32.230
20 Seconds later the first officer calls out
that they have reached 100 knots.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.800
6 seconds later the officer calls out V1.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.490
The point of no return.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.640
Aborting the take-off was no longer an option,
as there was not enough runway left to stop

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.640
in time.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.970
A mere 2 seconds later, a loud crash is heard
from the left-wing.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.479
And flames are seen billowing from the plane

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:54.210
crashing just moments later into a nearby
hotel.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:59.770
What followed was a multi-year air crash investigation,
unraveling the cause of the accident from

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.420
the wreckage using engineering.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.710
This is its story.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:13.870
Intro Sequence

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:19.260
The challenge facing air crash investigators
for any tragic loss like this is immense.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.760
The Concorde had crashed into a small hotel
and restaurant almost 10 km southwest of Charles

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.519
De Gualle airport.

00:02:26.519 --> 00:02:31.280
Flames had consumed the area around the crash
site, leaving the investigators a scattered

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.730
and scourched debris field to piece back together.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:39.750
The investigators started by organizing the
accident site to discover clues about what

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.200
had happened.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.280
Clipped trees north of the hotel crash site
suggested that the plane had crashed while

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.040
heading south.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.560
Following along that southward trajectory
the ruins of the Concorde’s powerful turbojet

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.130
engines were scattered on the darkened burnt
ground.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:00.630
The site was littered with large impact marks
with pieces of fuselage parts embedded in

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.040
the ground.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.230
Further south, the main cabin was found alongside
the tragic victims of the crash.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:11.110
To the east, lay the cockpit and its burned
instruments.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:15.060
Not all of the instruments survived the flames
and many of them were missing.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.310
However, a few instruments did survive the
crash and the heat.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:23.660
The investigators noted down the final reading
on the dials they could read.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:29.160
This would hopefully provide crucial information
that would lead to a revelation as the investigation

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.290
continued.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.600
As they dug through the remains of the aircraft
they found the flight data recorder.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.070
The tool that stores critical data about the
plane's flight record.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:43.069
They also found the cockpit voice recorder,
allowing the investigators to listen to the

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:44.910
plane’s final moments.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:49.580
In these audio recordings a large bang is
heard, shortly after the officer called out

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.640
V1.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.010
At the same moment, the

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:56.780
Flight data recorder displayed a sudden loss
of speed and thrust in engine number two on

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.140
the left wing, followed closely by its neighbour
engine 1.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.810
While engines 1 and 2 were struggling, the
data showed that the plane kept flying with

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:10.090
an almost constant angle of attack without
gaining altitude.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.030
While the loss of thrust on the left wing
caused the plane to roll.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:19.340
This flight data and the audio recording provided
a lot of evidence as to what had happened

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.240
but it left a crucial question unanswered.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.159
What was that loud noise that preceded the
fire and what caused it?

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:32.300
To find more clues the investigators turned
their attention to the runway.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:37.629
Even without the data recordings, the scorched
concrete painted a vivid picture of the Conchords

00:04:37.629 --> 00:04:38.729
final moments.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:43.689
It was clear from the burned grass and damaged
lights that the Concorde had lost control

00:04:43.689 --> 00:04:45.690
and veered to the left.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:51.759
While irregullar marks in the concrete, and
chunks of rubber pointed to a left tire failure.

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:57.030
Along the charred trail, the plane left more
breadcrumb clues crucial to continuing the

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:58.030
investigation.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:03.020
Besides the damaged rubber tire, two other
items stood out in the investigation.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:08.909
A mysterious titanium strip with irregular
drilled holes and an unscorched piece of the

00:05:08.909 --> 00:05:12.240
plane no larger than a sheet of printer paper.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.449
They were perplexed by that thin metal strip.

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:19.030
Irregular drill holes are not very common
in the industry.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:24.619
This led to the investigators trying to match
the 42 cm long strip to any component of the

00:05:24.619 --> 00:05:25.619
Concorde.

00:05:25.619 --> 00:05:30.060
But they couldn’t find a match for it anywhere
in the design documents.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:35.800
They concluded that this strip was not part
of the plane and had to be there before the

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:36.880
plane took off.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.610
Logically, the next step was to scrutinize
all of the departing flights on the same runway

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.279
to identify where this metal strip could have
come from.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:50.090
Flight 4590 left at 2:30 pm.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:54.060
They scoured through all of the maintenance
and the engineering documentation for any

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:57.400
plane that took off after 1:00 pm.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.639
One of the many papers they went through,
pointed them in the right direction.

00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:07.199
A McDonnell Douglas DC-10 that was servicing
a Continental flight that left a mere 5 minutes

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:13.430
before the Air France flight had a maintenance
document referencing a metallic wear strip.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:17.749
The maintenance documents showed that the
plane had undergone repairs of a metal wear

00:06:17.749 --> 00:06:21.089
strip just 1 month prior in Tel-Aviv.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:25.759
The same documents showed that the maintenance
work was not up to manufacturing standards.

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:30.740
To confirm the Investigators tracked down
the DC-10 and found it was missing a wear

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:34.270
strip with the same irregular drilled holes.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.229
The culprits' finger prints had been matched
to the scene of the crime.

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:44.369
The investigators concluded that a few minutes
before flight 4590 was due to depart, the

00:06:44.369 --> 00:06:47.830
Continental flight dropped its wear strip
on the runway.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:53.460
Crucially, a 32 centimeter long cut was also
found in the rubber tire debris left on the

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.560
runway.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:00.149
While it was tempting to connect this 42 centimeter
long metal strip and the cut found in the

00:07:00.149 --> 00:07:04.550
tire, the investigators could not assume it
was the cause.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.360
To confirm, investigators built a simulated
Concorde.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:15.099
A wagon modified to fit a new Concorde tire,
loaded with 25 tons to simulate the weight

00:07:15.099 --> 00:07:21.749
of the aircraft, and indeed the strip cut
the tire exactly as seen in the crash debris.

00:07:21.749 --> 00:07:28.210
However, this only answers why a tire would
burst, crucially this does not answer how

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:32.289
a tire burst would cause the eventual fiery
crash.

00:07:32.289 --> 00:07:36.990
After all, this crash was not the first time
the Concorde suffered a burst tire during

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.729
takeoff.

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:43.679
During its 30-year program, the Concorde experienced
57 tire malfunctions.

00:07:43.679 --> 00:07:48.979
22 of these events happened during take-off
and 19 were caused by a foreign object.

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:55.520
In 12 of these events structural damage was
seen, and 6 experienced fuel tank penetrations,

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.389
but none caused a fire like the one seen on
Flight 4590.

00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:02.849
One event in particular stands out.

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:09.969
On the 14th of June 1979, an Air France Concorde
took off from Dulles airport in Washington

00:08:09.969 --> 00:08:11.869
heading to Paris.

00:08:11.869 --> 00:08:17.020
During the take-off, one of the tires burst
and caused punctures in three of the tanks.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:20.949
This led to fuel being leaked at 4 kg/ sec.

00:08:20.949 --> 00:08:25.759
Luckily the crew noticed the damage and 24
minutes after takeoff, they landed safely

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:27.300
back at Dulles airport.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.300
[2]

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.399
In the report that followed this incident,
it was stated that the risk of fire was low

00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:34.550
after penetration of the tanks.

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:39.510
They stated that the low rate of fuel leakage
coupled with scarce ignition sources did not

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.789
pose a significant risk of fire.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:47.130
They also stated that even in the event of
a leak the location of the tanks would cause

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.850
the fuel to disperse rather than accumulate.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:56.550
The images and videos of the Concorde in flames
play in stark contrast to the findings from

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.690
the previous investigation.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.920
This of course begs the question.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.610
What was different about this flight that
led to this infamous picture.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:11.030
To answer that question, the investigators
needed to quantify the fuel leak rate of flight

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:12.740
4590.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:18.460
Visual evidence that came from videos and
images of the crash provided the first clues.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:23.140
Using these images the investigators could
estimate the dimensions of the flames trailing

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:25.110
behind the Concorde’s wing.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:31.100
Gauging the flame to be 50 metres long, 3
metres in diameter and 10 centimetres thick,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:37.970
they could calculate the rate of fuel leak
needed to sustain such a blaze at 60 kg/second.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.430
One of the instruments that survived the crash
confirmed this number.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:46.180
With the initial fuel load known, some simple
calculations confirmed the fuel leak at a

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:52.560
rate of 60 kg/s using the fuel dial, still
in its final position.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:59.700
15 times greater than the 4 kg/sec leak witnessed
in the previous incident at Dulles airport.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.880
This points to a very different fuel leak
mechanism.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.670
Here is where the third piece of evidence
from the runway helped the investors solve

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.210
the mystery of the crash.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:15.260
A single 30 cm by 30 cm piece of the tank
was found on the runway.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.280
This piece held the secrets to what happened.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:22.030
To an untrained eye this piece of metal wouldn’t
stand out.

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:26.680
Coated in the Concordes white heat-resistant
paint on one side, with strengthening struts

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.920
running along its length on the other.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.650
This was enough detail to pinpoint exactly
where in the plane it came from.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:37.090
Tank number 5, right above the landing gear.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:41.830
Next, the investigators noted that the piece
was not burned at all.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.760
Indicating it dropped before the fire started.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.190
To crack the final piece of the puzzle, the
investigators needed a materials science expert.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:55.060
To an untrained eye the fracture surface of
a metal tells you little, but to an expert

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.960
in fracture mechanics it tells a story.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.130
The investigators report noted that:

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:05.090
“the ruptures in the thin skins were matt
for the most part; the rupture face was angled

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:10.000
at 45°, which indicates that these were static
ruptures”

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.540
To understand the significance of this passage
we need some background on fracture mechanics.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:19.770
Why does a 45-degree rupture face indicate
a static rupture?

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:24.940
Let's run a couple of examples with different
materials undergoing different types of stress

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:28.160
and see how their fracture geometries vary.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:32.830
The investigators knew that the type of aluminum
being used in the tank was a ductile material.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:33.850
(Hiduminium, AU2GN, [4-5].

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:39.060
If we take a ductile material [6], and apply
stress to it, as its name implies the material

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.730
sample would deform as the load increases.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:46.350
[6]This leads to a phenomenon called necking
where the material thins out until it can't

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.550
stand a larger load and then fails.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.160
This leaves a characteristic 45-degree angle
on the failed piece.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:59.190
Often in these types of failures, you can
also see a cup and ball surface, again at

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.920
a 45-degree angle.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:05.390
These types of surfaces only happen to ductile
materials under tension.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:10.250
If another type of stress would be applied
to the sample, say torsional stress, then

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:13.670
the material would break at a 90-degree angle.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:19.380
Examining the fracture surface of this piece
told the experts working on this case a story.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:25.050
The hole in the tank wasn't caused by a puncture
by the tire physically piercing the tank.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:30.150
It pointed towards a rupture caused by a buildup
of pressure INSIDE the tank.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:34.860
The piece of rubber hit the underside of the
wing with enough force to cause a deformation

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:40.410
in the tank, causing the walls of the tank
to bend in while adjacent sections bulged

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:41.910
out.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:47.170
This was a serious problem because the fuel
was incompressible AND being the start of

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:49.220
the flight, the tanks were full.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:54.050
The resulting pressure wave slammed down into
the bulging sections and ejected the piece

00:12:54.050 --> 00:13:00.320
of tank found on the runway, and a hole this
large was capable of causing a fuel leak large

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.990
enough to match the flames seen in images
of the tragic accident.

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:06.220
The scene was set.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:11.480
A poorly positioned metal strip, caused a
stressed tire to burst and slam against the

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:16.540
underside of the wing, which in turn led to
a massive pressure wave inside the tank which

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:22.900
ruptured the tank, leading to a catastrophic
and tragic loss of power leading to the plane’s

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.600
uncontrollable descent.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.360
A decade-long legal battle ensued between
air France and Continental where they took

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.040
turns pointing fingers at each other.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:37.660
Finalizing in a 2010 court ruling that fined
Continental 1 million euros [7]

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:43.080
Plans were made to add Kevlar liners to the
fuel tanks to prevent further static ruptures

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:48.990
like this, and potential electrical ignition
sources were also moved and reinforced in

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.040
the area.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.500
New damage resilient tyres were also introduced.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:58.650
However, these modifications meant little
for the financially struggling concorde.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:03.200
This crash proved to be the final nail in
the coffin for the airline.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:07.620
With high ticket prices and low demand for
flights becoming even lower in the wake of

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.720
the crash, the Concorde proved to be financially
unviable.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:16.940
Leading both British Airways and Air France
to cancel their supersonic flying programs.

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:20.440
The end of an era marked by a tragic crash.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:25.970
I hesitated making this video for YouTube,
as it deals with a tragic accident and it’s

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:30.870
not always clear what will and won’t upset
the YouTube demonetisation system.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:36.320
That’s why I moved all my world war 2 related
content to Nebula, where I can continue making

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:42.100
